  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.LocalPath">Local path</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Mode.OnDemand">Download on demand (placeholders)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Mode.Always">Keep fully local</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.MinCopies">Evict only at … copies</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.Thumbs">Create thumbnails on demand</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.CopiesHint">Evicting means throwing bytes away. A file is evicted only once this many other nodes carry it completely. With a single server, 1 is right; with several nodes you may want 2. 0 turns the check off.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.TreeQuestion">What should appear at all?</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Share.SaveHint">Changes take effect when the share is restarted.</s:String>

  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.Title">Connect share</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.LocalFolder">Local folder</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.PathHint">This is where the folder appears in Explorer. The directory is created if it does not exist yet.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.ModeQuestion">How should the folder be kept?</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.TreeQuestion">What should be included?</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Accept.Hint">Branches you leave unchecked never appear — they cost neither space nor index.</s:String>

  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Title">Settings</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.CacheGroup">CACHE</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.CacheDir">Cache directory</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.CacheDirHint">This is where the cached files live — and they are the shares themselves: Windows fills the placeholders in place, and “evicting” means emptying them again. Not everything in here is cache: pinned files and folders in “keep fully local” mode stay. New shares land here; ones already connected stay where they are (folder settings).</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Budget">Cache budget (GB)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.MinFree">Minimum free space (GB)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LimitsHint">Two limits; whichever is reached first applies. When one is exceeded, whatever has gone unopened longest is evicted — across all folders. Pinned files and folders in “keep fully local” mode stay. For free space, 0 means no limit.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ClearCache">Clear cache now …</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.DataGroup">DATA</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.DataDir">Data directory</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.DataDirHint">Device certificate and index — the thumbnails live in the cache directory. A change takes effect at the next start; existing data does not move along, and without a certificate at the new place a new device ID is created.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.NetGroup">CONNECTIONS</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Listen">Accept calls from other devices</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ListenHint">Without this, SyncTClient only calls out. A remote device that has just added this device would never get through — and the “wants to connect” prompt would never appear. An unknown caller is shown to you before anything happens.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ListenPort">Port for incoming connections</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Parallelism">Parallel block requests</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LocalDiscovery">Call and listen on the local network</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LocalDiscoveryHint">A call into the local network every 30 seconds (UDP 21027), and an ear open for the calls of others. Without it this device stays invisible: a remote device whose address reads “dynamic” will not find it and never calls.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Announce">Tell the discovery server where this device can be reached</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.AnnounceHint">The same for outside the local network, signed with the device certificate. The server learns this line’s public IP address in the process.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Lookup">Ask the discovery server for remote devices without a fixed address</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.LookupHint">Needed for a remote device with no port open to the outside: it has no fixed address and instead tells the server how it can be reached. The server learns which device is being asked for — and your public IP address along the way.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Servers">Discovery servers (one per line)</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.ServersHint">The roles are in the address: “nolookup” only accepts announcements, “noannounce” only answers questions. For announcements there is one server each for IPv4 and IPv6 — which address a server sees depends on how it is reached.</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.Relays">Use relays when there is no direct route</s:String>
  <s:String x:Key="S.Settings.RelaysHint">Master switch. It can be turned off per remote device as well — Remote devices → Edit. A relay routes the connection through someone else’s machine; it stays encrypted, but it gets slow.</s:String>

